      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "# Lab: Cost Function for Healthcare Expense Prediction\n\n## The Global Healthcare Crisis\n\nRising healthcare costs are a defining challenge of the 21st century. In the United States alone, national health expenditure exceeds $4.5 trillion annually, accounting for roughly 18% of GDP. Globally, over 2 billion people face catastrophic out-of-pocket health spending that pushes them into poverty.\n\nInsurance companies, hospital administrators, and government agencies need reliable models to predict patient-level medical expenses. These predictions drive critical decisions:\n\n- **Insurance premium pricing** that must balance fairness and sustainability\n- **Hospital resource allocation** ensuring staff and equipment match projected demand\n- **Public health budgeting** for preventive interventions targeting high-risk populations\n- **Patient financial counseling** preparing individuals for expected out-of-pocket costs\n\nWhen predictions are inaccurate, the human toll is severe. Overestimates cause patients to avoid necessary care due to cost anxiety. Underestimates create financial ruin for families and fiscal instability for providers.\n\n## Goals\nIn this lab you will:\n- Implement and deeply understand the cost function J(w,b) for linear regression\n- Explore how model parameters affect prediction quality using healthcare data\n- Visualize cost surfaces in multiple dimensions and formats\n- Build a complete prediction pipeline with diagnostic analytics\n- Evaluate model limitations and ethical considerations in healthcare applications\n- Connect every mathematical step to real patient and provider outcomes\n\n## Notation Summary\n\n| Notation | Description | Python Variable |\n|:---------|:------------|:----------------|\n| $m$ | Number of patient records | `m` |\n| $x^{(i)}$ | Body Mass Index (BMI) for patient $i$ | `x_train[i]` |\n| $y^{(i)}$ | Annual medical expense for patient $i$ (in $1000s) | `y_train[i]` |\n| $w$ | Cost sensitivity to BMI (dollars per BMI unit) | `w` |\n| $b$ | Baseline medical expense at BMI=0 | `b` |\n| $f_{w,b}(x^{(i)})$ | Predicted expense for patient $i$ | `f_wb` |\n| $J(w,b)$ | Cost function (total prediction error) | `total_cost` |"
    },
