    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Problem Statement\n\n### Clinical Background\n\nBMI (Body Mass Index) is a widely used screening metric correlated with numerous health conditions including diabetes, cardiovascular disease, joint problems, and certain cancers. Higher BMI often corresponds to increased medical utilization: more doctor visits, specialist referrals, medications, and procedures.\n\nResearchers from a regional health network collected anonymized data from 8 patients, recording their BMI and total annual medical expenses.\n\n### Dataset: Patient Records\n\n| Patient | BMI | Annual Expense ($1000s) |\n|:--------|:----|:------------------------|\n| 1 | 22.0 | 2.1 |\n| 2 | 28.0 | 4.5 |\n| 3 | 32.0 | 5.8 |\n| 4 | 26.0 | 3.2 |\n| 5 | 35.0 | 7.1 |\n| 6 | 30.0 | 4.9 |\n| 7 | 24.0 | 2.8 |\n| 8 | 33.0 | 6.5 |\n\n**Clinical Note:** These expenses include all billed charges (insurance-paid plus patient out-of-pocket) for primary care visits, specialist consultations, emergency room visits, diagnostic tests, and prescription medications over one calendar year.\n\n**Why BMI?** We start with a single predictor for pedagogical clarity. In practice, expense models incorporate age, comorbidities, geographic location, insurance type, and many other factors. However, the cost function mechanics remain identical regardless of the number of predictors."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# SIMULATED HEALTHCARE DATASET\n# Inspired by patterns in the Medical Expenditure Panel Survey (MEPS)\n# Values are in 1000s of dollars for expenses and standard BMI units\n\nx_train = np.array([22.0, 28.0, 32.0, 26.0, 35.0, 30.0, 24.0, 33.0])\ny_train = np.array([2.1, 4.5, 5.8, 3.2, 7.1, 4.9, 2.8, 6.5])\npatient_ids = [f\"P{i+1}\" for i in range(len(x_train))]\n\nprint(\"PATIENT DATASET LOADED\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\nprint(f\"{'Patient':<10} {'BMI':<8} {'Annual Expense':<15} {'Category':<15}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 60)\nfor i in range(len(x_train)):\n    bmi_cat = \"Normal\" if x_train[i] < 25 else \"Overweight\" if x_train[i] < 30 else \"Obese\"\n    print(f\"{patient_ids[i]:<10} {x_train[i]:<8.1f} ${y_train[i]*1000:<10,.0f}      {bmi_cat}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 60)\nprint(f\"Total patients: {len(x_train)}\")\nprint(f\"BMI range: {x_train.min():.1f} - {x_train.max():.1f}\")\nprint(f\"Expense range: ${y_train.min()*1000:,.0f} - ${y_train.max()*1000:,.0f}\")\nprint(f\"Mean expense: ${np.mean(y_train)*1000:,.0f}\")",
      "comment": "Load simulated patient data. BMI categories follow WHO classification: Normal (18.5-24.9), Overweight (25-29.9), Obese (30+). Display formatting converts thousands-units to full dollar amounts for readability. Summary statistics provide context for the data distribution."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# VISUALIZATION 1: Comprehensive patient data overview\n\nfig = plt.figure(figsize=(16, 10))\ngs = GridSpec(2, 3, figure=fig, hspace=0.35, wspace=0.3)\n\n# --- Panel 1: Scatter plot of BMI vs Expense ---\nax1 = fig.add_subplot(gs[0, 0])\ncolors_bmi = ['#2ecc71' if b < 25 else '#f39c12' if b < 30 else '#e74c3c' for b in x_train]\nax1.scatter(x_train, y_train, c=colors_bmi, s=120, edgecolors='black', zorder=5)\nfor i, pid in enumerate(patient_ids):\n    ax1.annotate(pid, (x_train[i], y_train[i]), xytext=(x_train[i]+0.3, y_train[i]+0.15), fontsize=8)\nax1.set_xlabel('BMI')\nax1.set_ylabel('Annual Expense ($1000s)')\nax1.set_title('BMI vs Medical Expenses', fontweight='bold')\nax1.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\n# --- Panel 2: BMI distribution histogram ---\nax2 = fig.add_subplot(gs[0, 1])\nax2.hist(x_train, bins=5, color='#3498db', edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\nax2.axvline(x=np.mean(x_train), color='red', linestyle='--', linewidth=2, label=f'Mean={np.mean(x_train):.1f}')\nax2.set_xlabel('BMI')\nax2.set_ylabel('Frequency')\nax2.set_title('BMI Distribution', fontweight='bold')\nax2.legend()\nax2.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\n# --- Panel 3: Expense distribution histogram ---\nax3 = fig.add_subplot(gs[0, 2])\nax3.hist(y_train, bins=5, color='#9b59b6', edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\nax3.axvline(x=np.mean(y_train), color='red', linestyle='--', linewidth=2, label=f'Mean=${np.mean(y_train)*1000:,.0f}')\nax3.set_xlabel('Annual Expense ($1000s)')\nax3.set_ylabel('Frequency')\nax3.set_title('Expense Distribution', fontweight='bold')\nax3.legend()\nax3.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\n# --- Panel 4: Box plot by BMI category ---\nax4 = fig.add_subplot(gs[1, 0])\nnormal = y_train[x_train < 25]\noverweight = y_train[(x_train >= 25) & (x_train < 30)]\nobese = y_train[x_train >= 30]\nbox_data = [normal, overweight, obese]\nbox_labels = ['Normal', 'Overweight', 'Obese']\nbp = ax4.boxplot(box_data, labels=box_labels, patch_artist=True)\nbox_colors = ['#2ecc71', '#f39c12', '#e74c3c']\nfor patch, color in zip(bp['boxes'], box_colors):\n    patch.set_facecolor(color)\n    patch.set_alpha(0.6)\nax4.set_ylabel('Annual Expense ($1000s)')\nax4.set_title('Expenses by BMI Category', fontweight='bold')\nax4.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\n# --- Panel 5: Sorted bar chart ---\nax5 = fig.add_subplot(gs[1, 1:])\nsort_idx = np.argsort(y_train)\nsorted_pids = [patient_ids[i] for i in sort_idx]\nsorted_expenses = y_train[sort_idx]\nsorted_bmis = x_train[sort_idx]\nbar_colors = ['#2ecc71' if b < 25 else '#f39c12' if b < 30 else '#e74c3c' for b in sorted_bmis]\nbars = ax5.bar(range(len(sorted_pids)), sorted_expenses * 1000, color=bar_colors, edgecolor='black')\nax5.set_xticks(range(len(sorted_pids)))\nax5.set_xticklabels([f'{pid}\\n(BMI={b:.0f})' for pid, b in zip(sorted_pids, sorted_bmis)])\nax5.set_ylabel('Annual Expense ($)')\nax5.set_title('Patients Ranked by Medical Expense (with BMI)', fontweight='bold')\nax5.grid(True, alpha=0.3, axis='y')\n\n# Legend for BMI categories\nfrom matplotlib.patches import Patch\nlegend_elements = [\n    Patch(facecolor='#2ecc71', edgecolor='black', label='Normal (BMI < 25)'),\n    Patch(facecolor='#f39c12', edgecolor='black', label='Overweight (25-30)'),\n    Patch(facecolor='#e74c3c', edgecolor='black', label='Obese (BMI >= 30)'),\n]\nax5.legend(handles=legend_elements, loc='upper left')\n\nplt.suptitle('Healthcare Dataset: Comprehensive Overview', fontsize=16, fontweight='bold', y=0.98)\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Top-left: Scatter shows clear positive trend (higher BMI, higher expense)\")\nprint(\"Top-center/right: Distributions show spread of BMI and expense values\")\nprint(\"Bottom-left: Box plots reveal expense increases across BMI categories\")\nprint(\"Bottom: Sorted ranking shows individual patient positioning\")",
      "comment": "Five-panel comprehensive dashboard provides multiple perspectives on the same data. Scatter plot reveals the core relationship. Histograms show marginal distributions. Box plots compare groups statistically. Bar chart ranks individuals. Color coding by BMI category makes clinical categories immediately visible. This multi-view approach mirrors professional healthcare analytics dashboards."
