    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "## The Cost Function: Measuring Prediction Quality\n\n### Mathematical Foundation\n\nThe cost function quantifies how poorly our model performs. Every dollar of prediction error has consequences:\n\n- **Over-predicting** a patient's expenses may cause unnecessary anxiety, inflated insurance premiums, or denial of coverage\n- **Under-predicting** may leave families unprepared for bills and hospitals with budget shortfalls\n\nThe squared-error cost function:\n\nJ(w,b) = (1 / 2m) * sum from i=0 to m-1 of [f_wb(x^(i)) - y^(i)]^2\n\nwhere f_wb(x^(i)) = w * x^(i) + b\n\n**Why squaring matters in healthcare:**\n\nA prediction error of $500 affects a family differently than an error of $2,000. Squaring captures this nonlinear relationship: the $2,000 error is not 4 times worse, but 16 times worse in cost terms. This reflects how larger financial shocks create exponentially more disruption to household stability."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "def compute_cost(x, y, w, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the squared-error cost function for linear regression.\n    \n    Measures total prediction error across all patient records.\n    Lower cost = better predictions = more reliable healthcare planning.\n    \n    Args:\n      x (ndarray (m,)): BMI values for m patients\n      y (ndarray (m,)): Actual annual medical expenses in $1000s\n      w (scalar): Slope parameter (expense per BMI unit)\n      b (scalar): Intercept parameter (baseline expense)\n    \n    Returns:\n      total_cost (float): Average squared prediction error\n    \n    Interpretation:\n      Cost of 0.5 means average prediction error of approximately $1,000\n      (calculated as sqrt(2 * 0.5) * 1000 dollars)\n      This level of error could significantly impact patient financial planning.\n    \"\"\"\n    m = x.shape[0]\n    cost_sum = 0\n    for i in range(m):\n        # Predict expense using linear model\n        f_wb = w * x[i] + b\n        # Calculate squared error for this patient\n        error = f_wb - y[i]\n        cost = error ** 2\n        cost_sum += cost\n    total_cost = (1 / (2 * m)) * cost_sum\n    return total_cost\n\n\ndef compute_cost_detailed(x, y, w, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Enhanced cost function that also returns per-patient breakdown.\n    Useful for diagnostic analysis and targeted improvement.\n    \"\"\"\n    m = x.shape[0]\n    predictions = np.zeros(m)\n    errors = np.zeros(m)\n    squared_errors = np.zeros(m)\n    \n    for i in range(m):\n        predictions[i] = w * x[i] + b\n        errors[i] = predictions[i] - y[i]\n        squared_errors[i] = errors[i] ** 2\n    \n    total_cost = (1 / (2 * m)) * np.sum(squared_errors)\n    \n    return total_cost, predictions, errors, squared_errors",
      "comment": "Two versions of the cost function. The basic version matches the canonical formulation. The detailed version returns intermediate arrays for diagnostic analysis, allowing us to identify WHICH patients are hardest to predict for, which is clinically actionable information for improving data collection or model specification."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# SYSTEMATIC PARAMETER EXPLORATION\n\nprint(\"PARAMETER SPACE EXPLORATION\")\nprint(\"=\" * 80)\nprint(\"Testing multiple (w, b) combinations to understand the cost landscape.\")\nprint(\"=\" * 80)\n\n# Test a focused set of parameters informed by data inspection\n# From the data, expense increases roughly $200-300 per BMI unit\n# Baseline expense at BMI=0 should be near zero or slightly negative\nparam_tests = [\n    (0.1, 0.0, \"Minimal slope, no baseline\"),\n    (0.2, 0.0, \"Moderate slope, no baseline\"),\n    (0.3, -3.0, \"Steeper slope, negative baseline\"),\n    (0.25, -2.5, \"Balanced estimate\"),\n    (0.15, 1.0, \"Shallow slope, positive baseline\"),\n    (0.2, -1.5, \"Refined estimate\"),\n    (0.35, -5.0, \"Aggressive slope\"),\n    (0.05, 3.0, \"Very shallow slope\"),\n]\n\nprint(f\"\\n{'w':<8} {'b':<8} {'Cost J(w,b)':<14} {'Avg Error $':<14} {'Max Error $':<14} {'Assessment'}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 85)\n\nresults = []\nfor w_test, b_test, desc in param_tests:\n    cost = compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w_test, b_test)\n    _, preds, errs, _ = compute_cost_detailed(x_train, y_train, w_test, b_test)\n    avg_err = np.mean(np.abs(errs)) * 1000\n    max_err = np.max(np.abs(errs)) * 1000\n    \n    if cost < 0.3:\n        assessment = \"Good fit\"\n    elif cost < 1.0:\n        assessment = \"Acceptable\"\n    elif cost < 3.0:\n        assessment = \"Poor\"\n    else:\n        assessment = \"Unusable\"\n    \n    results.append((w_test, b_test, cost, avg_err, max_err, assessment, desc))\n    print(f\"{w_test:>6.2f}   {b_test:>6.1f}   {cost:>8.4f}       ${avg_err:>8,.0f}       ${max_err:>8,.0f}      {assessment}\")\n\n# Find best from our tests\nbest = min(results, key=lambda r: r[2])\nprint(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 85)\nprint(f\"Best tested: w={best[0]}, b={best[1]}, Cost={best[2]:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Average dollar error: ${best[3]:,.0f} per patient\")\nprint(f\"Description: {best[6]}\")\nprint(\"=\" * 85)",
      "comment": "Systematic exploration of parameter space. Average and maximum dollar errors translate abstract cost into concrete financial impact. Multiple assessment thresholds help develop intuition for what constitutes acceptable model performance in a healthcare finance context. The description column reminds us WHY each parameter combination was chosen."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# VISUALIZATION 2: Four-model comparison with error annotations\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(16, 12))\n\n# Select four contrasting parameter sets for comparison\nfour_models = [\n    {'w': 0.05, 'b': 3.0, 'label': 'Too Shallow (Under-sensitivity)'},\n    {'w': 0.2, 'b': -1.5, 'label': 'Near-Optimal Estimate'},\n    {'w': 0.35, 'b': -5.0, 'label': 'Too Steep (Over-sensitivity)'},\n    {'w': 0.15, 'b': 1.0, 'label': 'Moderate, Positive Baseline'},\n]\n\nfor idx, model in enumerate(four_models):\n    ax = axes[idx // 2][idx % 2]\n    w_t = model['w']\n    b_t = model['b']\n    \n    # Plot data points\n    ax.scatter(x_train, y_train, c=colors_bmi, s=120, edgecolors='black', zorder=5)\n    for i, pid in enumerate(patient_ids):\n        ax.annotate(pid, (x_train[i], y_train[i]), xytext=(x_train[i]+0.3, y_train[i]+0.15), fontsize=8)\n    \n    # Plot model line\n    x_line = np.linspace(20, 37, 100)\n    y_line = w_t * x_line + b_t\n    ax.plot(x_line, y_line, 'b-', linewidth=2, label=f'Model: w={w_t}, b={b_t}')\n    \n    # Draw prediction errors\n    cost_val, preds, errs, sq_errs = compute_cost_detailed(x_train, y_train, w_t, b_t)\n    \n    for i in range(len(x_train)):\n        pred = w_t * x_train[i] + b_t\n        ax.plot([x_train[i], x_train[i]], [y_train[i], pred], \n                color='red' if errs[i] > 0 else 'green', \n                linestyle='--', alpha=0.6, linewidth=1.5)\n    \n    # Calculate metrics for display\n    mae = np.mean(np.abs(errs)) * 1000\n    max_err = np.max(np.abs(errs)) * 1000\n    \n    ax.set_title(f\"{model['label']}\\nw={w_t}, b={b_t} | J={cost_val:.3f} | Avg Err=${mae:,.0f} | Max Err=${max_err:,.0f}\",\n                fontsize=10, fontweight='bold')\n    ax.set_xlabel('BMI')\n    ax.set_ylabel('Annual Expense ($1000s)')\n    ax.legend(loc='upper left', fontsize=9)\n    ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n    ax.set_xlim(20, 37)\n    ax.set_ylim(0, 10)\n\nplt.suptitle('Comparing Model Fits: Impact of Parameter Choice on Healthcare Predictions',\n             fontsize=14, fontweight='bold', y=0.98)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Red dashed lines: model OVER-predicts (predicted > actual)\")\nprint(\"Green dashed lines: model UNDER-predicts (predicted < actual)\")\nprint(\"Shorter dashes = better fit for that patient.\")\nprint(\"The near-optimal panel (top-right) minimizes total dashed-line length.\")",
      "comment": "Four-panel comparison lets learners see how different parameter choices create qualitatively different failure modes. Color-coded error lines (red for over-prediction, green for under-prediction) make the direction of error immediately visible. Displaying cost, average error, and max error together helps learners correlate the mathematical cost with intuitive financial impact."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# VISUALIZATION 3: 1D Cost Curve (varying w with fixed optimal b)\n\n# First, compute optimal parameters analytically using OLS\nx_mean = np.mean(x_train)\ny_mean = np.mean(y_train)\nw_opt = np.sum((x_train - x_mean) * (y_train - y_mean)) / np.sum((x_train - x_mean)**2)\nb_opt = y_mean - w_opt * x_mean\ncost_opt = compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w_opt, b_opt)\n\nprint(f\"ANALYTICALLY OPTIMAL PARAMETERS (OLS):\")\nprint(f\"  w = {w_opt:.4f} (expense increase per BMI unit)\")\nprint(f\"  b = {b_opt:.4f} (baseline expense at BMI=0)\")\nprint(f\"  Cost J = {cost_opt:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"  Approximate average error: ${np.sqrt(2*cost_opt)*1000:,.0f}\")\n\n# Plot cost as function of w (with b fixed at optimal)\nw_range = np.linspace(-0.1, 0.6, 300)\ncosts_w = [compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w, b_opt) for w in w_range]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(16, 6))\n\n# Left: Cost vs w\nax1 = axes[0]\nax1.plot(w_range, costs_w, 'b-', linewidth=2)\nax1.plot(w_opt, cost_opt, 'g*', markersize=20, zorder=5, label=f'Optimum: w={w_opt:.3f}')\nax1.fill_between(w_range, costs_w, alpha=0.1, color='blue')\n\n# Highlight zones\nax1.axvspan(0.0, 0.1, alpha=0.05, color='orange')\nax1.axvspan(0.35, 0.6, alpha=0.05, color='red')\nax1.axvspan(0.15, 0.30, alpha=0.05, color='green')\n\nax1.set_xlabel('w (dollars per BMI unit, in $1000s)')\nax1.set_ylabel('Cost J(w, b_opt)')\nax1.set_title('Cost vs Slope Parameter (b fixed at optimal)', fontweight='bold')\nax1.legend(fontsize=10)\nax1.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nax1.text(0.02, max(costs_w)*0.7, 'Underestimating\\nsensitivity', fontsize=9, color='orange')\nax1.text(0.37, max(costs_w)*0.7, 'Overestimating\\nsensitivity', fontsize=9, color='red')\nax1.text(0.17, max(costs_w)*0.4, 'Good range', fontsize=9, color='green')\n\n# Right: Cost vs b (with w fixed at optimal)\nb_range = np.linspace(-8, 5, 300)\ncosts_b = [compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w_opt, b) for b in b_range]\n\nax2 = axes[1]\nax2.plot(b_range, costs_b, 'r-', linewidth=2)\nax2.plot(b_opt, cost_opt, 'g*', markersize=20, zorder=5, label=f'Optimum: b={b_opt:.3f}')\nax2.fill_between(b_range, costs_b, alpha=0.1, color='red')\nax2.set_xlabel('b (baseline expense in $1000s)')\nax2.set_ylabel('Cost J(w_opt, b)')\nax2.set_title('Cost vs Intercept Parameter (w fixed at optimal)', fontweight='bold')\nax2.legend(fontsize=10)\nax2.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\nplt.suptitle('One-Dimensional Cost Curves: How Each Parameter Affects Prediction Quality',\n             fontsize=13, fontweight='bold', y=1.01)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Left: Varying slope (sensitivity) while holding intercept optimal.\")\nprint(\"  Too shallow = misses how strongly BMI drives expenses.\")\nprint(\"  Too steep = exaggerates the effect, causing alarm.\")\nprint(\"Right: Varying intercept while holding slope optimal.\")\nprint(\"  Too high baseline = predicts excessive minimum costs.\")\nprint(\"  Too low (negative) = predicts negative expenses for healthy patients (unrealistic).\")",
      "comment": "Analytical OLS solution provides the ground truth optimum for comparison. One-dimensional cost curves (varying one parameter at a time) are easier to interpret than 2D surfaces. Zone shading with labels connects mathematical regions to clinical interpretation. Filled area under curve emphasizes the bowl shape visually."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# VISUALIZATION 4: 3D Cost Surface and Contour Map\n\n# Generate dense grid of parameter values\nw_vals = np.linspace(-0.1, 0.6, 120)\nb_vals = np.linspace(-8, 5, 120)\nW_grid, B_grid = np.meshgrid(w_vals, b_vals)\n\n# Compute cost for entire grid\nJ_grid = np.zeros_like(W_grid)\nfor i in range(W_grid.shape[0]):\n    for j in range(W_grid.shape[1]):\n        J_grid[i, j] = compute_cost(x_train, y_train, W_grid[i, j], B_grid[i, j])\n\nfig = plt.figure(figsize=(18, 7))\n\n# --- 3D Surface ---\nax1 = fig.add_subplot(131, projection='3d')\nsurf = ax1.plot_surface(W_grid, B_grid, J_grid, cmap='viridis', alpha=0.85, edgecolor='none')\nax1.scatter(w_opt, b_opt, cost_opt, color='red', s=200, marker='*', zorder=10, label='Global Minimum')\nax1.set_xlabel('w (slope)', fontsize=10)\nax1.set_ylabel('b (intercept)', fontsize=10)\nax1.set_zlabel('J(w,b)', fontsize=10)\nax1.set_title('3D Cost Surface', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nax1.view_init(elev=30, azim=45)\nax1.legend(fontsize=9)\n\n# --- 2D Contour ---\nax2 = fig.add_subplot(132)\nlevels = np.linspace(0, 20, 25)\ncf = ax2.contourf(W_grid, B_grid, J_grid, levels=levels, cmap='viridis')\ncs = ax2.contour(W_grid, B_grid, J_grid, levels=levels, colors='black', linewidths=0.3, alpha=0.4)\nax2.plot(w_opt, b_opt, 'r*', markersize=20, zorder=5, label=f'Minimum: w={w_opt:.2f}, b={b_opt:.2f}')\n\n# Mark our tested parameter combinations\ntested_params = [(0.05, 3.0), (0.2, -1.5), (0.35, -5.0), (0.15, 1.0)]\nfor w_t, b_t in tested_params:\n    c_t = compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w_t, b_t)\n    ax2.plot(w_t, b_t, 'wo', markersize=8, markeredgecolor='black', zorder=5)\n    ax2.annotate(f'J={c_t:.1f}', (w_t, b_t), xytext=(w_t+0.02, b_t+0.3), fontsize=7, color='white')\n\nplt.colorbar(cf, ax=ax2, label='Cost J(w,b)')\nax2.set_xlabel('w (slope)')\nax2.set_ylabel('b (intercept)')\nax2.set_title('2D Contour Map', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nax2.legend(fontsize=9)\n\n# --- Per-patient error breakdown at optimal ---\nax3 = fig.add_subplot(133)\n_, opt_preds, opt_errs, opt_sq_errs = compute_cost_detailed(x_train, y_train, w_opt, b_opt)\n\nbar_colors = ['#e74c3c' if e > 0 else '#2ecc71' for e in opt_errs]\nbars = ax3.barh(range(len(patient_ids)), opt_errs * 1000, color=bar_colors, edgecolor='black')\nax3.set_yticks(range(len(patient_ids)))\nax3.set_yticklabels([f'{pid}\\nBMI={x_train[i]:.0f}' for i, pid in enumerate(patient_ids)])\nax3.axvline(x=0, color='black', linewidth=1)\nax3.set_xlabel('Prediction Error ($)')\nax3.set_title('Per-Patient Errors at Optimum', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\nax3.grid(True, alpha=0.3, axis='x')\n\nfrom matplotlib.patches import Patch\nerr_legend = [\n    Patch(facecolor='#e74c3c', edgecolor='black', label='Over-prediction'),\n    Patch(facecolor='#2ecc71', edgecolor='black', label='Under-prediction'),\n]\nax3.legend(handles=err_legend, fontsize=9)\n\nplt.suptitle('Complete Cost Function Analysis for Healthcare Expense Model',\n             fontsize=15, fontweight='bold', y=1.01)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(f\"Optimal model: expense = {w_opt:.3f} * BMI + ({b_opt:.3f})\")\nprint(f\"Minimum cost: J = {cost_opt:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"This means each additional BMI unit adds approximately ${w_opt*1000:,.0f} in annual expenses.\")\nprint(f\"Even at optimum, individual predictions can be off by up to ${np.max(np.abs(opt_errs))*1000:,.0f}.\")\nprint(f\"This residual error reflects factors beyond BMI: genetics, lifestyle, access to care, etc.\")",
      "comment": "Three-panel figure provides the complete cost analysis. 3D surface shows the bowl shape. Contour map shows parameter landscape with tested points overlaid. Per-patient error bar chart reveals WHICH patients the model struggles with, directing attention to where additional features might help. Red bars show over-predictions (model says higher expense than reality), green shows under-predictions."
