      "source": "# VISUALIZATION 5: Gradient Descent Simulation (preview of next lab)\n\n# Simulate gradient descent to preview the next lab concept\n# This demonstrates HOW we find the minimum automatically\n\ndef compute_gradients(x, y, w, b):\n    \"\"\"Computes partial derivatives of cost w.r.t. w and b.\"\"\"\n    m = x.shape[0]\n    dj_dw = 0\n    dj_db = 0\n    for i in range(m):\n        err = (w * x[i] + b) - y[i]\n        dj_dw += err * x[i]\n        dj_db += err\n    dj_dw /= m\n    dj_db /= m\n    return dj_dw, dj_db\n\n# Run gradient descent\nw_gd = 0.05  # Start with a poor guess\nb_gd = 3.0\nalpha = 0.001  # Learning rate\nnum_iterations = 200\n\nhistory_w = [w_gd]\nhistory_b = [b_gd]\nhistory_cost = [compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w_gd, b_gd)]\n\nfor iteration in range(num_iterations):\n    dj_dw, dj_db = compute_gradients(x_train, y_train, w_gd, b_gd)\n    w_gd = w_gd - alpha * dj_dw\n    b_gd = b_gd - alpha * dj_db\n    \n    history_w.append(w_gd)\nhistory_b.append(b_gd)\nhistory_cost.append(compute_cost(x_train, y_train, w_gd, b_gd))\n\nprint(\"GRADIENT DESCENT SIMULATION (Preview)\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\nprint(f\"Starting point: w=0.05, b=3.0 (poor guess, cost={history_cost[0]:.3f})\")\nprint(f\"After {num_iterations} iterations:\")\nprint(f\"  w = {w_gd:.4f} (true optimal: {w_opt:.4f})\")\nprint(f\"  b = {b_gd:.4f} (true optimal: {b_opt:.4f})\")\nprint(f\"  Cost = {history_cost[-1]:.4f} (true optimal: {cost_opt:.4f})\")\nprint(f\"\\nThe algorithm automatically descended the cost bowl!\")\n\n# Plot gradient descent trajectory\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(18, 5))\n\n# Trajectory on contour\nax1 = axes[0]\ncf = ax1.contourf(W_grid, B_grid, J_grid, levels=np.linspace(0, 20, 25), cmap='viridis')\nax1.plot(history_w, history_b, 'r.-', linewidth=1.5, markersize=3, label='Descent Path')\nax1.plot(w_opt, b_opt, 'g*', markersize=20, zorder=5, label='True Minimum')\nax1.plot(history_w[0], history_b[0], 'yo', markersize=12, zorder=5, label='Start')\nax1.set_xlabel('w')\nax1.set_ylabel('b')\nax1.set_title('Gradient Descent Trajectory', fontweight='bold')\nax1.legend(fontsize=8)\n\n# Cost over iterations\nax2 = axes[1]\nax2.plot(range(len(history_cost)), history_cost, 'b-', linewidth=2)\nax2.axhline(y=cost_opt, color='g', linestyle='--', label=f'Optimal Cost={cost_opt:.4f}')\nax2.set_xlabel('Iteration')\nax2.set_ylabel('Cost J(w,b)')\nax2.set_title('Cost Reduction Over Iterations', fontweight='bold')\nax2.legend(fontsize=9)\nax2.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nax2.set_yscale('log')\n\n# Parameter convergence\nax3 = axes[2]\nax3.plot(range(len(history_w)), history_w, 'b-', linewidth=2, label=f'w (target={w_opt:.3f})')\nax3.axhline(y=w_opt, color='b', linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\nax3.plot(range(len(history_b)), history_b, 'r-', linewidth=2, label=f'b (target={b_opt:.3f})')\nax3.axhline(y=b_opt, color='r', linestyle='--', alpha=0.5)\nax3.set_xlabel('Iteration')\nax3.set_ylabel('Parameter Value')\nax3.set_title('Parameter Convergence', fontweight='bold')\nax3.legend(fontsize=9)\nax3.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\nplt.suptitle('Gradient Descent: Automatically Finding the Minimum Cost',\n             fontsize=14, fontweight='bold', y=1.02)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"Left: Red path shows gradient descent navigating the cost landscape.\")\nprint(\"Center: Cost decreases rapidly at first, then slows as it approaches minimum.\")\nprint(\"Right: Both parameters converge to their optimal values simultaneously.\")",
      "comment": "Gradient descent preview bridges this lab (cost function) with the next (optimization). The trajectory plot overlays the descent path on the contour map, making the bowl-rolling analogy concrete. Log-scale cost plot reveals the exponential convergence rate. Parameter convergence chart shows that w and b converge at different rates due to different gradient magnitudes, motivating the need for learning rate tuning."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# FINAL APPLICATION: Healthcare Planning with Optimized Model\n\nprint(\"HEALTHCARE EXPENSE PREDICTION SYSTEM\")\nprint(\"=\" * 65)\nprint(f\"Model: expense = {w_opt:.3f} * BMI + ({b_opt:.3f})\")\nprint(f\"Optimal cost: J = {cost_opt:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Interpretation: Each BMI unit adds approximately ${w_opt*1000:,.0f}/year\")\nprint(\"=\" * 65)\n\n# Predict for hypothetical new patients\nnew_patients = [\n    (20.0, \"Healthy young adult\"),\n    (27.0, \"Overweight, middle-aged\"),\n    (34.0, \"Obese, seeking wellness program\"),\n    (40.0, \"Severely obese, high-risk patient\"),\n    (23.0, \"Active, normal BMI\"),\n]\n\nprint(f\"\\n{'Patient Profile':<35} {'BMI':<6} {'Predicted':<12} {'Confidence':<10}\")\nprint(\"-\" * 65)\nfor bmi, profile in new_patients:\n    pred = w_opt * bmi + b_opt\n    # Confidence based on distance from training data centroid\n    dist_from_centroid = abs(bmi - np.mean(x_train))\n    if dist_from_centroid < 2:\n        conf = \"High\"\n    elif dist_from_centroid < 5:\n        conf = \"Medium\"\n    else:\n        conf = \"Low (extrapolation)\"\n    print(f\"{profile:<35} {bmi:<6.1f} ${pred*1000:<8,.0f}   {conf}\")\n\nprint(\"-\" * 65)\nprint(\"\\nIMPORTANT CAVEATS:\")\nprint(\"1. Predictions for BMI=40 are EXTRAPOLATIONS beyond training data range.\")\nprint(\"   Extrapolation is risky: the linear relationship may not hold.\")\nprint(\"2. Cost of 0.2 means average error of about $632 per prediction.\")\nprint(\"3. This model uses BMI ONLY. Real expense models need age, sex,\")\nprint(\"   comorbidities, geography, insurance status, and more.\")\nprint(\"4. Ethical concern: BMI-based pricing could discriminate against\")\nprint(\"   individuals with genetic or medical conditions affecting weight.\")\nprint(\"5. This model is for EDUCATIONAL purposes and must not be used\")\nprint(\"   for actual insurance pricing or clinical decisions.\")",
      "comment": "Final application demonstrates practical use while emphasizing limitations. Confidence indicator based on distance from training centroid introduces the concept of model applicability domain. Extensive caveats address ethical concerns: extrapolation danger, omitted variable bias, discrimination risk, and scope limitations. These caveats are essential for responsible ML practice in healthcare."
