    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Key Takeaways\n\n### Mathematical\n\n1. **The cost function J(w,b) provides a single number measuring model quality.** Lower cost means predictions better match reality, which in healthcare translates directly to financial planning accuracy.\n\n2. **Squared error creates a convex bowl.** This guarantees one findable minimum, making optimization reliable. The bowl shape also means small parameter deviations from optimal produce proportionally small cost increases, providing some robustness.\n\n3. **Per-patient error analysis reveals model blind spots.** Identifying which patients are hardest to predict guides feature engineering: perhaps adding age or comorbidity indicators would reduce those specific errors.\n\n4. **Gradient descent automates optimization.** Rather than manually searching parameter space, algorithms trace the cost surface downhill to the minimum, enabling scalability to datasets with millions of patients.\n\n### Healthcare and Ethical\n\n1. **Predictive accuracy has direct financial consequences.** An average error of $600 per patient seems small, but across millions of insured patients, it represents hundreds of millions in misallocated funds.\n\n2. **Single-variable models are insufficient for healthcare.** BMI alone explains much of the variance, but real expense drivers include dozens of interacting factors. The residual cost at the optimum quantifies what BMI cannot explain.\n\n3. **Models can encode and amplify bias.** Using BMI as a pricing factor could penalize individuals whose weight reflects medical conditions (thyroid disorders, medication side effects) rather than lifestyle choices.\n\n4. **Extrapolation beyond training data is dangerous.** Predicting expenses for BMI=40 when our highest training example is 35 risks dramatic errors if the true relationship bends upward at extreme obesity.\n\n### Preview: Multivariate Extension\n\nThe next lab extends to multiple features (BMI + age + blood pressure + ...) using multivariate linear regression. The cost function structure remains identical, but the parameter space becomes higher-dimensional. Gradient descent becomes essential because closed-form solutions become computationally expensive for large feature sets."
